B-0 (Boosty) audit round 3, F1 + F2 (the libc half) -- poll() gives a connected
AF_UNIX socket the stream-socket shape: a peer's orderly close is POLLIN|POLLHUP,
not POLLHUP|POLLERR.

The kernel half is not in this series: until 2026-09-21 the kernel sampled the
SERVER's end of a /srv byte connection for a client too (a client was
"readable" while its own unread request sat in the ring) and walked no hook
list when a reply arrived, so a client's poll() never woke. That is fixed in
kernel/srvconn.c + kernel/devsrv.c + kernel/poll.c (ARCH 23.3) and is what makes
poll() on a pouch socket a readiness source at all.

What is left for libc is the SHAPE. The kernel reports such a connection
pipe-like, identically for both endpoints: POLLIN means bytes are buffered, a
drained EOF is POLLHUP without POLLIN, and because teardown closes both
directions at once POLLHUP always arrives with POLLERR. The native 9P servers
are written to that. A program written to sockets is written to Linux's: a
peer's orderly close reads POLLIN|POLLHUP (read() will not block; it returns
0), and POLLERR means a pending socket error, which an orderly close is not.
The loop every port has --

    if (pfd.revents & POLLIN) { n = read(fd, ...); if (n == 0) peer_closed(); }

-- never sees its POLLIN on POLLHUP alone, and spins on a poll() that returns
at once, for ever. Event libraries that test POLLERR first tear the connection
down as failed and drop the reply bytes still in the ring.

So for a CONNECTED AF_UNIX slot, and only there, POLLHUP gains the POLLIN and
POLLOUT the caller asked for and sheds POLLERR. POLLOUT because Linux's
unix_poll reports a stream socket writable after the peer's close -- its source
says so, "this prevents stuck sockets" -- and a writer waiting on
POLLOUT|POLLERR, having lost the POLLERR here, would otherwise spin on a bare
POLLHUP instead of writing and meeting the close in the write's error (B-0
audit round 4 F4). This is the boundary line's job, done once here: the
alternative is every port's poll loop, or nothing. Not touched:
AF_INET slots (they poll netd's ready file, whose contract is netd's), and a
LISTENING slot's POLLHUP (the service was tombstoned -- not an EOF).

NOT COVERED, and recorded: an ACCEPTED AF_UNIX socket. accept() returns the
kernel handle untagged (0006's design: the server side stays on the raw
read/write fast path), so poll() cannot tell it from a pipe without a kernel
query per fd per call, and a pouch SERVER still sees the pipe-like row when its
client closes. It folds into the small-integer-socket-fd redesign 0039 records
as owed (an fd -> slot side table covers accepted sockets too). One in-tree
pouch server does poll an accepted socket: stratumd's fs_pool
(pool_socket_pending) makes a zero-timeout poll(POLLIN) on each connection to
decide whether a client has pipelined, and counts ANY revents -- POLLHUP and
POLLERR included -- as "pending", so the pipe-like row changes nothing for it:
the dispatched op proceeds and the reader meets the EOF on its next header
read. (An earlier draft of this header said no in-tree server polled an
accepted socket; round 4 F5 found this one.)

ppoll() reaches this through poll() (0039); select() refuses socket fds.

Device-side pin (same commit, not in this patch file): pouch-hello-sockets,
after the server closes, ppoll()s the client socket and requires EXACTLY
POLLIN|POLLHUP, then an fgetc() of EOF. Before it, sequenced by barriers so
that no schedule can turn it green over a broken kernel: the client polls only
AFTER the server has consumed its request, the server replies, and the client
must see EXACTLY POLLIN with the connection still open.

